{"access":"public","aliases":[],"assertions":[{"claim_ids":["clm_16b2ac86a400ecfc2abd2ad751c23e0c6067d6307ea85e230d1e6aabf09b0b65"],"opposing_claim_ids":[],"text":"Agents can drive Herdr through a CLI and socket API to spawn panes, prompt each other, and wait until another agent is blocked."},{"claim_ids":["clm_1830d8daed69ad935906c2dacc7147ecb9e35d3b5cadcfb9dd848ea54631162e"],"opposing_claim_ids":[],"text":"The TUI supports tmux-style prefix keys plus mouse click, drag, and split interactions."},{"claim_ids":["clm_217402aa2d8e51ffed9849a6e4850a2b576d04d99095f4151368e72d585d7c62"],"opposing_claim_ids":[],"text":"After a server or machine restart, Herdr restores layout and can resume supported agent sessions, but the original processes do not survive."},{"claim_ids":["clm_2df600ab73b950089bd3a58b6414ce963c27ec834ef17a37d1c0fb87152bba84"],"opposing_claim_ids":[],"text":"Herdr is described as a single Rust binary with no Electron dependency, running inside the user's existing terminal."},{"claim_ids":["clm_92210d92405ff1624b4d399a4ae581d9850e4833f2728906b5e8edc5996c5bfd"],"opposing_claim_ids":[],"text":"Each pane is marked working, blocked, or idle, and Herdr signals when an agent stops and needs input."},{"claim_ids":["clm_ba62e3b07c1ec94ee50962ce29afc060a248fc218d3e5e906c14fe9f0f9bfb4d"],"opposing_claim_ids":[],"text":"The product keeps terminals running in a background server so work continues after the client closes or SSH drops, and restores saved layout after server or machine restart."},{"claim_ids":["clm_f3ce657bedc76bb15260ef489db1ebaadb3ba3d47c9ab3158fe92d81ebe2f2b8"],"opposing_claim_ids":[],"text":"Herdr runs agents like Claude Code, Codex, Cursor, OpenCode, and Grok in their own terminals without wrapping or replacing them."}],"claim_ids":["clm_16b2ac86a400ecfc2abd2ad751c23e0c6067d6307ea85e230d1e6aabf09b0b65","clm_1830d8daed69ad935906c2dacc7147ecb9e35d3b5cadcfb9dd848ea54631162e","clm_217402aa2d8e51ffed9849a6e4850a2b576d04d99095f4151368e72d585d7c62","clm_2df